**New findings**

1. **N1 — High — The standing E2E rule blocks T1 before the runner exists.**  
   **Location:** [Standing rules](E:/projects/virtual-office/spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md:16), 
[T24](E:/projects/virtual-office/spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md:90).

   Every E2E check must run “through the T24 runner.” T1 already requires regression E2E to pass before completion, as 
do subsequent server slices. Execution cannot reach T24 until T1–T6 finish. The mechanical dependency check misses 
this prose prerequisite.

   **Smallest fix:** explicitly allow T1–T6’s existing regression checks through the current local runner, then 
require T24 for subsequent E2E gates and the final Chrome/Edge runs. Alternatively, move the base runner prerequisite 
ahead of T1 and defer fixtures that require new database objects.

2. **N2 — High — Generation-less compatibility requests need the same atomic fence exclusion.**  
   **Location:** [D4 planning 
decisions](E:/projects/virtual-office/spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md:19), 
[T2](E:/projects/virtual-office/spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md:47).

   T2 expressly requires the commit-time generation check **“with”** the new fields. Its compatibility branch only 
says generation-less requests are rejected when the key “already has fence state.”

   A route-level interpretation permits this race: a generation-less create passes its absence check; its insert is 
delayed; a fence reports not stored; the outbox item is cancelled or expires; the delayed legacy path inserts 
afterward. Composite uniqueness prevents two rows under that key, but cannot prevent this late delivery—or duplicate 
content after cancellation and rewriting under a fresh key. The listed AC-045 tests exercise generated attempts, 
without expressly testing this branch.

   **Smallest fix:** require generation-less insertion admission to participate in the same atomic, commit-time 
exclusion as fencing. Add held-request and blocked-insert tests where the generation-less request loses to a fence. 
Define whether ordinary key registration counts as “fence state,” and test stored-message legacy replays so 
registration does not accidentally break Phase 4 retry.

   Safe legacy compatibility implements §10. Allowing a fenced insert to commit, or weakening legitimate Phase 4 
replay compatibility, changes the approved contract; D4’s delegation of small choices does not authorize that.

3. **N3 — Medium — T25’s probe can pass without establishing the fault capabilities its consumers require.**  
   **Location:** [T25 Task/Done 
when](E:/projects/virtual-office/spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md:101).

   The probe requires an unhealthy/closed target for each stream, without requiring **error, timeout, and terminal 
closure for each stream**. It could pass using only join timeouts, leaving T13/T15 unable to run their other mandatory 
scenarios.

   Playwright can connect to the real server and selectively intercept messages, so multiplexing itself is manageable. 
Closing `WebSocketRoute` closes a socket side, affecting all its channels. Dropping ordinary topic events does not 
itself produce an unhealthy subscription status. [Playwright WebSocketRoute 
documentation](https://playwright.dev/docs/api/class-websocketroute).

   Topic-specific error/closure needs a Phoenix protocol fault mechanism or actual server channel termination; 
`phx_error` and `phx_close` are channel messages. [Supabase protocol 
documentation](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/realtime/protocol).

   **Smallest fix:** make the probe a three-stream × three-failure-class matrix, recording actual subscription status 
and continued delivery on unaffected streams. Identify the protocol/server mechanism allowed under §15; preserve real 
joins, application callbacks, and transport rather than setting application health directly. If synthetic protocol 
failure frames exceed the accepted network-shaping policy, resolve that boundary explicitly.

4. **N4 — Medium — T27 authorizes redispatch after every not-stored fence result.**  
   **Location:** [T27](E:/projects/virtual-office/spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md:128), 
[T9](E:/projects/virtual-office/spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md:146).

   T27 applies fencing before cancellation, expiry, or redispatch, then unconditionally states that “a not-stored 
result is redispatched once.” For an unknown item past its delivery window, that instruction sends the item that 
T9/FR-009 must expire. It also conflicts with fencing performed to enable cancellation.

   **Smallest fix:** state that a not-stored result permits the requested transition. Redispatch occurs only while the 
item remains eligible, current, and within its delivery window; cancellation and expiry complete without a create. Add 
an unknown-but-not-stored expiry case alongside the existing stored-but-unknown case.

The fail-closed **429** choice itself does not contradict FR-008 or BR-005, provided refusal occurs before storing or 
fencing. Metrics-budget refusal is also compatible with FR-028 when messaging remains unaffected. Those choices do not 
inherently require a new owner decision.

T9 after T10 is appropriate. T17 needs T14 for its stale-poll mention check. T19 now names FR-009, but the remaining 
R8 fix is to add an expired manual retry whose emitted latency demonstrably uses the original send time to T19’s 
completion evidence.

T24 is checkable through its probe. T25 is checkable in principle once N3 is tightened; its AC-015 `Covers` entry 
supports the harness prerequisite, while T13/T15 own actual recovery acceptance. No additional unowned AC clause was 
found after accounting for standing rules and T22’s composite checks.

